#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157073 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157445 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d04ce85-c364-52ba-aa30-ca47e307496e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations in a Complete Case"}] :question/id #uuid "af260af2-fd05-54bc-8f6f-a5612cb922db" :question/math-academy-id "e-7048" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation ![](images/a8/a8ea187557f2a8104688a946913572d8cd62b579c7d24dd91e4cafbfb61f9874.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n![](images/b2/b2e09fc2bc82576d702ebc07440760f80b8db1e3607888e469c3661c25ad3371.svg)\nthe formula for the general solution is\n![](images/8a/8a19755a74fe3d8c582c2408c2d802411fce6449d0e9c00bac00de8d8ca91826.svg)\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n![](images/1b/1b43b7c5f8459588632917502c96bb792a86a9f8e6c3a2d67eda71c6ce56f496.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157074 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157446 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c19f9d9-fa4b-5261-b9a0-99a203aeac30" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations in a Defective Case"}] :question/id #uuid "f1eaa2b8-544c-5217-82a5-8ec0a311c5a8" :question/math-academy-id "e-7049" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n![](images/d6/d66051007ab78d2727f3b501156878a369a04b24bca337ed587feb07935a1de7.svg)\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n![](images/bf/bfd809b436c24efcb2e9934b1c36293dfecb4bf87ab41c05f8cbbb9d506ea34d.svg)\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix ![](images/7e/7e191da3b082da420622418962e7491a6e78b6ed8192259b9b50ec8040bbc525.svg)\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda=-4$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n![](images/fb/fb833263882adc2422083c111e96b0e030263f93e4c0174082f0f57667b46e8e.svg)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](images/fe/fe33e849a0e3baa7e8547c9559f9c36e980b8e715cba6e26900eea5d815e9b30.svg)\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda$ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $\\mathbf v,$ the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}  + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\, ),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nTo find $\\mathbf{w},$ we solve the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{v}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-4+4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4+4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n![](images/2c/2c443847a5559737066873593b399117c03e9c8141b403bc7f820c9ad493e2a9.svg)\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\nw_2 = 1.\n$$\n\nThe variable $w_1$ is a free variable. Setting $w_1 = 0,$ we get \n![](images/00/00b635d3858d38c41ad9e1bacb00ed4c6370b5e2112aa82faa6ef37a83209a6c.svg)\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n![](images/d1/d1911a797bfa5915fb1478c91dc007ca3f1419b1093180dfb08932a008337b6e.svg)\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0, b=1,$ and $a+b=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157075 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157447 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "109cc543-253b-5f18-b13b-e54b99e8b12a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem in a Defective Case"}] :question/id #uuid "38f7a261-b58c-5ce1-ab77-6bfb5ce40b19" :question/math-academy-id "e-7050" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n![](images/be/be4b1ac82a25533e28028ad0aade71e2dd2614c5cd302d777a90a9f16be845c1.svg)\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda=3.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix ![](images/df/dfddfc4e82f21cd490aff75bbdc6fbece98cd05a5cc70a1148492697faa8741a.svg)\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda=3$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n![](images/92/92c6c6be816658e065ebba1079a2c7fa031cc9137ffa8dec7bf6e498e69195f3.svg)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](images/75/75524e426e894f50985740367c96f128dfef1e216ae6969a529889cf22803bc7.svg)\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda$ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $\\mathbf v,$ the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1  \\mathbf{v}e^{\\lambda t} + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}t e^{\\lambda t} +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nTo find $\\mathbf{w},$ we solve the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{v}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-1-3 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 7-3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n![](images/bb/bb892c2fd7b1a2873b9a7427d95599f67fbd10e47ec6fd2cb377b9f6f45f4185.svg)\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\nw_1 =\\dfrac{1}{2} - 2 w_2 .\n$$\n\nThe variable $w_2$ is a free variable. Setting $w_2 = 0,$  we get $w_1 = \\dfrac{1}{2},$ and therefore\n![](images/46/4696fe4ee3fd46a639fb465c10aa2ac44e608a0cf59ca6c1a210eb4861b3dd5b.svg)\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n![](images/05/0525d47967c203832ff4deea0931e007cc7127f24d248cd8265765ad95708367.svg)\n\nNow, we need to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial condition ![](images/48/48da1d81a447aa78c3ffa68ad5f089bc0d8e4bac5fa489ddad2747055b0f2a99.svg) Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}-2(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(4t-3){e}^{3t} \\\\ (1-2t){e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157737 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157446 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c19f9d9-fa4b-5261-b9a0-99a203aeac30" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations in a Defective Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e2f4047-28db-5982-bf27-62bd47c9eb17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300185} {:answer/id #uuid "42e4a570-7f1b-547f-b489-c83eaeafeeac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300290} {:answer/id #uuid "4b99766b-da02-5b51-a022-313d44a16524" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300354} {:answer/id #uuid "7f8e3287-39f8-532f-b6b0-a87ef89feb4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300595} {:answer/id #uuid "e27d740d-be6d-5d25-b6a0-e68ad85f227e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301086}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f8e3287-39f8-532f-b6b0-a87ef89feb4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300595} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2e08cf9-f383-515d-a856-21fa02542b1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f38771d6-22f6-588d-95e5-d42dd40123e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-325120" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince $A$ is lower-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2-2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2-2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 1{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0,b=1,$ and $a+b=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530517 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157446 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c19f9d9-fa4b-5261-b9a0-99a203aeac30" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations in a Defective Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6123a8e7-8566-539c-a378-251b6266a004" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529818} {:answer/id #uuid "afc1305e-c6de-5db9-8835-27482372478c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530019} {:answer/id #uuid "dd60fa24-2113-573f-927a-cc5cc09eff1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530126} {:answer/id #uuid "f239d215-8bad-5494-ad9f-a7e4dc4827bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530182} {:answer/id #uuid "ffb02d5f-3761-5753-87fa-efd0f4de2d82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530214}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffb02d5f-3761-5753-87fa-efd0f4de2d82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530214} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ffbd89f-d981-524e-9095-fdb0dc1768f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c42bb2d4-33f1-5601-a069-ea5fef63539f" :question/math-academy-id "q-326237" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{4t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =4$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4-4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4-4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5{w}_{2}=1 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{2}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{1}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{1}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{4t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0,b=\\frac{1}{5},$ and $a+b=\\frac{1}{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302268 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157447 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "109cc543-253b-5f18-b13b-e54b99e8b12a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem in a Defective Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09b833ac-6e99-5f04-9aea-02a1d1abf1cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\,{e}^{5t} \\\\ (2-6t){e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299983} {:answer/id #uuid "30e58490-02de-57c1-a235-c5f21bc66f4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\,{e}^{5t} \\\\ 2\\,{e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300197} {:answer/id #uuid "3f187179-b0f8-5d94-91aa-4179df6e4772" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\,{e}^{5t} \\\\ (2+12t)\\,{e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300272} {:answer/id #uuid "848d9602-7869-51e2-9531-be656bfae476" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}(-3-12t){e}^{5t} \\\\ 2\\,{e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300623} {:answer/id #uuid "dea94c08-d4a8-50f4-b269-2013de24496f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\,{e}^{5t} \\\\ (2-12t)\\,{e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301066}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dea94c08-d4a8-50f4-b269-2013de24496f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\,{e}^{5t} \\\\ (2-12t)\\,{e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301066} :answer-field/id #uuid "9741ff31-5e13-50b4-8cd7-e54eafbd4045" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301713}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "26bb41ab-79e8-5c9f-95c2-ae20713f031a" :question/math-academy-id "q-326239" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince $A$ is lower-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}5-5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5-5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 4{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{c}_{2}}{4} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{c}_{2}}{4} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}\\,t{e}^{5t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\,{e}^{5t} \\\\ (2-12t)\\,{e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157783 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157445 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d04ce85-c364-52ba-aa30-ca47e307496e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations in a Complete Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e9a8c81-a091-545d-84fc-e0dd993ec18b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345621} {:answer/id #uuid "434dca2b-bb1e-5b6a-a0fc-484d397d614c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345932} {:answer/id #uuid "581d572d-6189-5f30-8179-ad07a3ead556" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346102} {:answer/id #uuid "6c04748c-785f-5be6-8f54-c3dd0b0f7a20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346288} {:answer/id #uuid "ef0a53e5-676f-5cb8-9cd4-65e20571d1dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}\\,{c}_{1}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "581d572d-6189-5f30-8179-ad07a3ead556" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346102} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d1be337-4b04-5f96-942e-67689c075869" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c1a7a31a-a1ff-52db-b87c-67b3aaf6bbd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-80548" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $\\,{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x(t).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530472 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157445 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d04ce85-c364-52ba-aa30-ca47e307496e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations in a Complete Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bba6a2c-47a1-5b62-98ad-01578dba4908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-4t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{t}^{2}{e}^{-4t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529599} {:answer/id #uuid "1b168faa-7cfe-5aab-9d7c-288148211462" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529633} {:answer/id #uuid "440ab6ac-9050-5cbf-8d75-c724eb062d9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529738} {:answer/id #uuid "785d1da2-9cd4-54fc-b182-e308cb8c3a12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529887} {:answer/id #uuid "c08d5be1-ef64-5ba5-adca-0ebd93c83b4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530061}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "440ab6ac-9050-5cbf-8d75-c724eb062d9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529738} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcb4ea53-f6ce-50a3-91c6-7ce13b2fa771" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530380}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6cd0f9e1-610c-5585-9b7b-d28d8445a663" :question/math-academy-id "q-80599" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $\\,{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]x(t).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda \\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348419 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157446 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c19f9d9-fa4b-5261-b9a0-99a203aeac30" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations in a Defective Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f8430ae-f2df-5901-af99-08b9afe18d9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346325} {:answer/id #uuid "955c6786-041f-56d1-a8a2-ce05b9f436cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346654} {:answer/id #uuid "98ba621e-111e-5691-b61d-7fcf4899d462" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346683} {:answer/id #uuid "c75a9cd4-47b9-5ee1-a928-ce3a55928be3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347084} {:answer/id #uuid "ea629320-0d6b-58a0-800f-b0f90fa23465" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347420}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c75a9cd4-47b9-5ee1-a928-ce3a55928be3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347084} :answer-field/id #uuid "f114ab66-f9f7-54e4-9082-d4ad0a1947a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348028}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fa98e78d-c213-5ff9-b494-b8ef24528f71" :question/math-academy-id "q-80604" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$\n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda =-1.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-1$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1+1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -3+1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{w}_{1}+4{w}_{2} &=-2 \\\\ -1{w}_{1}-2{w}_{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=-1-2{w}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get ${w}_{1}=-1,$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=-2,b=-1,$ and $a+b=-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413832 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157446 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c19f9d9-fa4b-5261-b9a0-99a203aeac30" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations in a Defective Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dfffefc-7dc4-503a-9b1f-a84d08149c77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413826} {:answer/id #uuid "672f41ac-64c5-58b5-868f-7c1ad340ab36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413827} {:answer/id #uuid "9fa05af9-3949-5d3f-84eb-7ccc92942416" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413828} {:answer/id #uuid "cc7ac799-30a1-5efa-892d-6a6142f8760c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413829} {:answer/id #uuid "fb961936-d8ed-5956-ad00-aabdf66df6d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413830}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc7ac799-30a1-5efa-892d-6a6142f8760c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413829} :answer-field/id #uuid "9eaed666-f3ff-5e76-bdcf-86e2bff8289c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413831}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9318e8aa-b34c-5ef4-bb58-533fb20cecad" :question/math-academy-id "q-80606" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{t}+\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$\n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda =1.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =1$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-2-1 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 4-1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3{w}_{1}+9{w}_{2} &=3 \\\\ -1{w}_{1}+3{w}_{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=3{w}_{2}-1.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get ${w}_{1}=-1,$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=3,b=-1,$ and $a+b=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530452 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157447 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "109cc543-253b-5f18-b13b-e54b99e8b12a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem in a Defective Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2466ac54-a28c-5e81-8865-e657fdfdddf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t} \\\\ -{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529657} {:answer/id #uuid "4e8a7671-a4f9-5cb6-aad5-295efc3dd7c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(1+2t){e}^{-2t} \\\\ (2t-1){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529768} {:answer/id #uuid "6015db17-0453-5d51-9f6c-9b63c750a7e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(1+t){e}^{-2t} \\\\ -{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529813} {:answer/id #uuid "7d81a220-377a-5acf-8093-4fcf21b38c96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(1+t){e}^{-2t} \\\\ -(1+2t){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529896} {:answer/id #uuid "f5ea50a5-4a7b-5fa7-80c3-71388673c51f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(1+\\frac{t}{2}){e}^{-2t} \\\\ (2t-1){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530188}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6015db17-0453-5d51-9f6c-9b63c750a7e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(1+t){e}^{-2t} \\\\ -{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529813} :answer-field/id #uuid "15c8b6c9-4d90-5de4-bd62-0285c561ccb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a2a9a41-3861-52b6-aa0d-2940105366a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-80616" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-2$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2+2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2+2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}-1{w}_{2}=1 \\\\ 0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{2}=-1.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{1}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{1}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ -{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ -{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(1+t){e}^{-2t} \\\\ -{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530500 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157447 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "109cc543-253b-5f18-b13b-e54b99e8b12a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem in a Defective Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31af8209-d6f7-5861-bb8f-000ec6cd874e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}(t-1){e}^{5t} \\\\ 2{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529690} {:answer/id #uuid "6bed8f55-cb39-5a3b-86c2-5cd8c26c256c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{5t} \\\\ (2+t){e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529854} {:answer/id #uuid "a90db176-5114-5ca8-a22e-ebcadf82891a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}(t-1) \\\\ (2-t){e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529998} {:answer/id #uuid "b6ce04dc-14c5-5800-b05b-94c7bf45b6c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}(2t-1){e}^{5t} \\\\ (t+2){e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530038} {:answer/id #uuid "d8570b37-63eb-524c-ba0e-1892354b0a05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530118}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6bed8f55-cb39-5a3b-86c2-5cd8c26c256c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{5t} \\\\ (2+t){e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529854} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d46ab32-dd48-56fe-af69-855cd332f49c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a4894a4b-386a-5db4-8e7b-0e56f6d08b5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-80617" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5-5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 5-5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0 \\\\ -1{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=-1.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{5t}. \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{5t} \\\\ (2+t){e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530419 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157445 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d04ce85-c364-52ba-aa30-ca47e307496e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations in a Complete Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "827bb430-5de2-5c2c-852f-2f50416777f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t/3},\\,{c}_{1}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529901} {:answer/id #uuid "99ddea77-e1d5-55f5-a432-e6303cb4cb21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t/3}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t/3},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529958} {:answer/id #uuid "a2cb13b6-b0d6-5d1c-b058-05a7573d9e6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t/3}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t/3},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529983} {:answer/id #uuid "a7622813-e693-5336-bdfb-7190ca932526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t/3},\\,{c}_{1}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529996} {:answer/id #uuid "bfa84c58-35f5-52bb-a6b3-d6cd3ea0ee3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530059}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99ddea77-e1d5-55f5-a432-e6303cb4cb21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t/3}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t/3},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529958} :answer-field/id #uuid "08fed612-7f25-5560-bb83-15a960a92bfa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0512b677-42e3-54b0-95e1-80a717c5187c" :question/math-academy-id "q-80908" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $\\,{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]x(t).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda \\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t/3}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t/3},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351184 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157445 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d04ce85-c364-52ba-aa30-ca47e307496e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations in a Complete Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d117cfb-9ce1-5683-a949-6f515598da8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t/3}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t/3},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348559} {:answer/id #uuid "0fad2b61-e6bd-574c-84b1-bc11573f711f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t/3}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t/3},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348586} {:answer/id #uuid "5ee3cc99-27ce-54ac-b2cc-df80279fefd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t/2}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t/2},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349319} {:answer/id #uuid "9b286870-dec6-5b57-94e2-e00e743ecb97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t/2}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t/2},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349829} {:answer/id #uuid "d5299cf7-b192-51a3-8af7-6d8c62e4f4ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t/3}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t/3},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350316}] :answ